rsh -- Command

Restricted command interpreter
/usr/bin/rsh [-ceiknpstuvx] [file [argument ...]]
sh -r [-ceiknpstuvx] [file [argument ...]]

rsh is the restricted Bourne shell.  It is the same program as sh -- the
same file, built from the same source -- and behaves in every way like sh
except that, once the profiles have run, it refuses five things:

	cd, with or without an argument.

	Setting PATH or SHELL, by any means: a plain assignment, an
	assignment prefixed to a command, export NAME=value, read NAME,
	or a for loop over that name.

	Any command name that contains a `/'.

	Output redirection: `>' and `>>', on a command or on exec.

	newgrp.  This one is not part of the historical restricted shell
	and is here because of what COHERENT's newgrp ends by doing: on
	SUCCESS it execs /bin/sh as a LOGIN shell, which reads the profiles
	again and is therefore a fresh, unrestricted shell -- and it is a
	builtin that runs /bin/newgrp by absolute pathname, so no PATH
	could keep it out of reach.  A failure no longer gets one: every
	error path in newgrp now reports and exits non-zero, so a group
	that does not exist, an access list that does not name you or a
	wrong group password leaves you where you were.  That narrows the
	hole, it does not close it -- a group you ARE entitled to is still
	a one-word way out -- so the refusal stays.  login is NOT refused:
	it asks for credentials, and it is how a confined user ends the
	session.

A refusal prints `Restricted: ' followed by what was refused, sets a
non-zero status, and executes nothing.  Everything else -- input
redirection, here documents, `>&' and `<&' descriptor duplication, pipes,
background commands, all other variables and all other builtins -- works
exactly as it does in sh.

rsh restricts itself because of the NAME it was invoked under: an argv[0]
of `rsh', with any leading `-' and any directory prefix removed.  Invoking
sh with the -r option does the same thing.  There is no way to turn the
restriction off from inside: `set' refuses every flag past -x at run time,
and it has no `+' form, so a restricted shell stays restricted, and so does
every subshell it forks.

WHEN THE RESTRICTIONS BEGIN
The restrictions take effect only AFTER /etc/profile and $HOME/.profile
have run.  That ordering is the point of the feature.  The administrator's
profile builds the environment the user is to be confined to -- above all
the PATH -- and it could not do that under a shell that already refused to
set PATH or to cd.  Commands in those two files therefore run unrestricted;
they are the last unrestricted commands the session executes.  A shell that
is not a login shell has no profile to run and restricts from its first
command.

Because .profile runs unrestricted, it must not be writable by the user it
confines.  A user who can edit their own .profile is not restricted at all.
The same goes for the home directory that holds it, and for /etc/profile.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT PROTECT AGAINST
A restricted shell is a fence, not a cell.  It confines a cooperative user
to a menu; it does not contain a hostile one.  Read this list before giving
anyone rsh as a login shell.

PATH is the whole mechanism.  Refusing `/' in a command name means the user
can run only what PATH names, so a PATH left pointing at /bin and /usr/bin
restricts nothing worth restricting.  A restricted account's profile must
set PATH to a directory holding only commands chosen for it, and that
directory must not be writable by the user.

sh itself must not be on that PATH.  `sh' is a command name with no `/' in
it; if PATH can find it, the user types it and gets an unrestricted shell,
because a new sh is not invoked as `rsh' and has no -r.  For the same
reason do not put rsh's own directory on the PATH under a second name.

Any command that runs another command hands the user an unrestricted shell.
Every one of these was checked in this distribution and does:

	ed		`!command'
	elvis (vi)	`:!command' and `:shell'
	less		`!command'
	awk		`print | "command"'
	mail		`!command' and `~!command'
	find		-exec command

and so does anything that starts $SHELL or $EDITOR.  None of them can be
made safe by rsh; they must be kept off the restricted PATH.  An editor is
the one people forget.  This is not a list of bugs in those commands: a
restricted shell restricts the SHELL, and has never claimed otherwise.

Commands that write files defeat the redirection rule.  Refusing `>' stops
the shell from creating a file; it does not stop cp, cat, tee, dd, ed, or
any editor from doing the same thing.  Redirection is refused so that a
confined user cannot overwrite a file by accident or by shell syntax alone,
not because writing has been made impossible.

Commands that change directory defeat the cd rule the same way, and a
command run with a relative pathname argument can still reach anywhere the
user has permission to go: rsh restricts the command NAME, never its
arguments.  `cat ../../etc/passwd' is not refused and is not meant to be.

`. file' is not restricted, and its argument may contain a `/'.  Sourcing a
file is not an escape -- the commands in it run under the same restrictions
-- but it does let a user run a script they wrote elsewhere.

The restrictions are the shell's, so they end where the shell ends.  Any
program the user starts is an ordinary process with the user's own
privileges; it can chdir, write files, and exec anything on the disk.  File
permissions, not rsh, are what stop that.

The historical restricted shell has always had these limits.  They are
listed here because a restricted shell is worth using only when someone has
read them and chosen the PATH accordingly.

SETTING UP A RESTRICTED ACCOUNT
Name rsh in the last field of the account's /etc/passwd entry:

	guest::100:1:Guest:/usr/guest:/usr/bin/rsh

Make a directory holding only the commands that account may run, and read
WHAT THIS DOES NOT PROTECT AGAINST before deciding what goes in it:

	mkdir /usr/restricted
	cp /bin/echo /bin/cat /bin/ls /bin/date /usr/restricted
	chmod 755 /usr/restricted

Point the account's profile at it, and at nothing else:

	echo PATH=/usr/restricted > /usr/guest/.profile

Then take the profile, the home directory, and the command directory away
from the user, because a user who can write any of the three is not
restricted at all:

	chown root /usr/guest /usr/guest/.profile /usr/restricted
	chmod 755 /usr/guest /usr/restricted
	chmod 644 /usr/guest/.profile

FILES
/usr/bin/rsh		the restricted shell (the same file as /bin/sh)
/etc/profile		run unrestricted at login
$HOME/.profile		run unrestricted at login

SEE ALSO
commands, login, passwd, sh

DIAGNOSTICS
`Restricted: X' -- the shell refused X.  The command did not run and the
exit status is non-zero.

NOTES
Earlier releases of this system shipped /usr/bin/rsh as a byte-identical
copy of /bin/sh built from a duplicate of the shell sources.  It accepted
-r, recorded it, and restricted nothing: an account given that rsh as its
login shell had a fully unrestricted shell.  There is now one set of shell
sources and two names for one binary.

This is not the BSD remote shell, which post-dates it and collides with the
name.  The remote shell is remsh on this system.
